# deploy/secrets/rclone.conf.example — copy to deploy/secrets/rclone.conf
# (chmod 600) and fill in your remote's own credentials, or leave it
# empty and copy it verbatim if BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE (deploy/.env) is a
# bare local path (a drill, or a deliberately same-disk backup — see
# docs/runbooks/backup-restore.adoc for why that's not sufficient on its
# own for production).
#
#   cp deploy/secrets/rclone.conf.example deploy/secrets/rclone.conf
#   chmod 600 deploy/secrets/rclone.conf
#
# The file must exist before `docker compose up` — `deploy/docker-compose.yml`'s
# `backup` service mounts it as a `configs:` reference, which fails
# `docker compose up` outright if the source file is missing (see that
# service's own comment). An empty file (this one, uncommented) is a
# valid, working rclone config for the local-path case above; it is not
# a placeholder that silently does nothing.
#
# rclone's own config format — `rclone config` (interactive) writes this
# for you, or write a remote by hand. Example for an S3-compatible
# bucket (uncomment and fill in for a real remote):
#
# [my-remote]
# type = s3
# provider = AWS
# access_key_id = ...
# secret_access_key = ...
# region = ...
